<p>
  Cargo has no single "index URL" environment variable the way pip/uv (
  <code>PIP_INDEX_URL</code>/<code>UV_INDEX_URL</code>) and Go (
  <code>GOPROXY</code>) do -- pointing it at kellnr requires a real{" "}
  <code>~/.cargo/config.toml</code>
  source-replacement block, written automatically for both the Docker and
  OrbStack VM engines. pip also refuses a plain-HTTP index on any host other
  than <code>localhost</code>/<code>127.0.0.1</code> unless it's explicitly
  trusted via <code>PIP_TRUSTED_HOST</code>, which both engines set
  automatically alongside <code>PIP_INDEX_URL</code>
  wherever the index isn't reached via <code>localhost</code>.
</p>

<h2 id="orbstack-vm-disk-caching">OrbStack VM Local Disk Caching</h2>
<p>
  The proxies above cache package <em>downloads</em> over the network. Package
  managers also keep a local, already-extracted disk cache (<code>~/.npm</code>,{" "}
  <code>~/.cache/pip</code>, <code>~/go/pkg</code>,
  <code>~/.cargo/registry</code>, etc.), which for the Docker engine is a plain{" "}
  <code>-v host:container</code>
  bind mount. An OrbStack VM is a real, separate guest filesystem with no such
  flag, but every non-isolated OrbStack VM automatically virtiofs-shares the
  entire host macOS filesystem into the guest at a fixed path,
  <code>/mnt/mac&lt;absolute-macOS-path&gt;</code>. RunZero's OrbStack driver
  uses this to bind-mount each host-side cache directory onto its
  container-style destination path inside the VM before the job runs, so cache
  data written by one ephemeral VM is really on host disk and visible to the
  next VM cloned for the same architecture.
</p>

<h2 id="job-environment">
  Job Environment &amp; the <code>RUNZERO</code> Marker
</h2>
<p>
  Every runner, on every backend, exports the same variables before
  <code>run.sh</code>, so every job step inherits them, the way GitHub's own
  images set theirs. Each one fixes a cache miss that was measured on real jobs:
</p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
  <table class="comparison-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Variable</th>
        <th>Value</th>
        <th>Why</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>RUNNER_TOOL_CACHE</code>, <code>AGENT_TOOLSDIRECTORY</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>/opt/hostedtoolcache</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Without them <code>actions/setup-node</code> and <code>setup-go</code>
          download into the throwaway <code>_work/_tool</code> instead of the
          host-backed tool cache.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>npm_config_registry</code>, <code>pnpm_config_registry</code>,
          <code>YARN_REGISTRY</code>, <code>YARN_NPM_REGISTRY_SERVER</code>
        </td>
        <td>Verdaccio</td>
        <td>
          pnpm 11 ignores every <code>npm_config_*</code> variable and pnpm 10
          reads only those, so both spellings are set.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>pnpm_config_store_dir</code>, <code>npm_config_store_dir</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>~/.local/share/pnpm/store</code>
        </td>
        <td>The pnpm store is a host cache mount.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>~/.cache/ms-playwright</code>
        </td>
        <td>Browsers are cached on the host, per architecture.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>RUNZERO</code>, <code>RUNZERO_CACHES</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>1</code>, list of mounted caches
        </td>
        <td>Lets a workflow tell it is running on RunZero.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>
  On RunZero, <code>actions/cache</code> steps for <code>node_modules</code>,
  the pnpm store or Playwright browsers only duplicate, slowly and over the
  network, what the host already caches. Skip them with the marker; the same
  workflow keeps caching normally on GitHub-hosted runners:
</p>
<pre class="code-block"><code>{markerExample}</code></pre>
<p>
  If a job still needs <code>apt-get</code>, it goes through apt-cacher-ng. The
  runner images keep their package indices and skip translation files, so
  <code>apt-get update</code> fetches only what changed since the image was
  built. Repositories served over HTTPS (Microsoft, Docker) bypass the proxy.
</p>
<p>
  Run <code>make doctor</code> to check all of this from inside a fresh runner.
  For each backend it reports the registry and store that npm, yarn and pnpm
  10/11 resolve, the tool cache and Playwright mounts, proxy latency, cache
  sizes, host load, runner sizing, and whether the bridge and autoscaler run the
  code you have checked out.
</p>

<h2 id="native-arch-override">Native-Arch Override (Apple Silicon)</h2>
<p>
  A workflow that targets <code>runs-on: [self-hosted, local, amd64]</code>
  has to keep working on GitHub-hosted <code>ubuntu-latest</code>. On Apple
  Silicon, every such job runs under Rosetta emulation, which is several times
  slower for Node and browsers, even though most jobs (Node, pnpm, Playwright,
  Go) do not depend on the architecture.
</p>
<p>
  With <code>NATIVE_ARCH_OVERRIDE=all</code> (or a list of
  <code>owner/repo</code> names) RunZero serves those repositories'
  <code>amd64</code> jobs with native arm64 runners, with no change to the
  workflow. The runner still carries the job's <code>amd64</code> label, but
  <code>RUNNER_ARCH</code> inside it is <code>ARM64</code>, so
  <code>setup-node</code> and Playwright fetch native binaries. x86-64 binaries
  and <code>--platform linux/amd64</code> containers still run through Rosetta.
  The startup log, the job log line and the dashboard's routing card show when
  an override was applied.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Trade-off:</strong> a job that checks <code>uname -m</code>, or builds
  amd64 artifacts without <code>--platform</code>, gets arm64. Leave such
  repositories off the list. Jobs labelled <code>rosetta</code> or
  <code>x86_64</code> are never moved, and the setting does nothing on amd64
  hosts.
</p>
